#{[17592186101946 1029 "The **average value** of a continuous function on a closed interval $[a,b]$ is given by\n\n$$\nf_\\textrm{avg} = \\dfrac{1}{b-a}\\int_a^b f(x) \\: \\textrm{d}x\n$$"] [17592186106921 1029 "Recall that the distance between vertices $u$ and $v$ in an unweighted undirected graph, $d(u,v)$, is the length (i.e., the number of edges) of the shortest path from $u$ to $v$. If no path from $u$ to $v$ exists, we say that the distance is $\\infty$.\n\nIn a simple undirected unweighted graph $G=(V,E)$ with $n$ vertices $v_1, v_2, \\ldots, v_n$, the **distance matrix** $D$ of the graph is an $n \\times n$ matrix (table), where the entry $a_{ij}$ at the intersection of the $i$th row and the $j$th column equals the *distance* between the vertices $v_i$ and $v_j$.\n\nNotice that, according to this definition:\n\n- If no path exists between $v_i$ and $v_j$, then the entry $a_{ij}$ is $\\infty$.\n- If $i=j$ (i.e., it is the same vertex), then the entry $a_{ii}$ is $0$.\n- The distance matrix of an undirected graph is symmetric about the main diagonal.\n\nLet's look at an example of how to build the distance matrix for the graph below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1392/Source/Images/573f222dae5b9df7c51804281128625a.png)\n\nWe start with a square $4 \\times 4$ matrix with $0$s on the main diagonal and examine every pair of vertices to determine the remaining matrix entries.\n\n- The path $v_2-v_3-v_4$ has two edges and is the shortest path between $v_2$ and $v_4$. Hence, we write $2$ in the entry $a_{24}$ and, by symmetry, in the entry $a_{42}$.\n\n|  | $v_1$ | $v_2$ | $v_3$ | $v_4$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $v_1$ | $0$ |  |  |  |\n| $v_2$ |  | $0$ |  | $2$ |\n| $v_3$ |  |  | $0$ |  |\n| $v_4$ |  | $2$ |  | $0$ |\n\n- Since the vertices $v_2$ and $v_3$ are adjacent, the shortest path between them is $1$. Hence, we write $1$ in the entry $a_{23}$ and, by symmetry, in the entry $a_{32}$. Similarly, since $v_3$ and $v_4$ are adjacent, we write $1$ in the entry $a_{34}$ and $a_{43}$.\n\n|  | $v_1$ | $v_2$ | $v_3$ | $v_4$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $v_1$ | $0$ |  |  |  |\n| $v_2$ |  | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| $v_3$ |  | $1$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n| $v_4$ |  | $2$ | $1$ | $0$ |\n\n- Since the vertex $v_1$ is isolated, the distance from it to any other vertex is $\\infty$. Hence, we write $\\infty$ in every entry of the $1$st row and in every entry of the $1$st column, except for $a_{11}$.\n\n|  | $v_1$ | $v_2$ | $v_3$ | $v_4$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $v_1$ | $0$ | $\\infty$ | $\\infty$ | $\\infty$ |\n| $v_2$ | $\\infty$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| $v_3$ | $\\infty$ | $1$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n| $v_4$ | $\\infty$ | $2$ | $1$ | $0$ |\n\nThe distance matrix is completed. In regular matrix notation, we can write this distance matrix as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & ∞ & ∞ & ∞ \\\\ ∞ & 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ ∞ & 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ ∞ & 2 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186106922 1029 "Before we proceed, we first need to state a theorem that's often abbreviated to **CPCTC**:\n\n> *Corresponding parts of congruent triangles are congruent.*\n\nWhen two triangles are shown to be congruent using a congruence criterion, CPCTC allows us to conclude that all corresponding angles and sides of those triangles are congruent. This theorem is often used in the final step of a geometric proof after establishing triangle congruence.\n\nTo demonstrate, consider the following congruent triangles.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1357/Source/Images/7f130758c28dd7c8924078eda40b798c.png)\n\nWe won't prove this formally, but it's easy to see that since\n$\\angle A\\cong \\angle K, \\qquad \\overline{AB}\\cong\\overline{KL},\\qquad \\angle B\\cong \\angle L$\nthen $\\triangle ABC\\cong \\triangle KLM$ by the ASA criterion.\n\nNow that triangle congruence has been established, we can use CPCTC to list *all* congruent sides and angles:\n\nFor example:\n\n- According to CPCTC, the following statements about segment congruence must be true:\n$\\overline{AC}\\cong\\overline{KM}, \\qquad \\overline{BC}\\cong\\overline{LM}$\nWhile ${\\color{black}{\\overline{AB}\\cong\\overline{KL}}}$ is also true, this was given, and so we don't need to use CPCTC to establish it.\n\n- Also, according to CPCTC, we have\n$\\angle C \\cong \\angle M$.\nAgain, since $\\angle A\\cong \\angle K$ and $\\angle B\\cong \\angle L$ were *given*, CPCTC wasn't needed to establish these.\n\nLet's now look at a formal proof that uses CPCTC."] [17592186106923 1029 "A **tree** is an undirected connected simple graph with no cycles; a graph with no cycles is **acyclic**.\n\nA **forest** is an undirected simple graph with no cycles. Equivalently, a forest is an undirected simple graph whose connected components are trees, hence the name.\n\nA tree with $N$ vertices always has exactly $N-1$ edges. This provides a useful criterion for identifying trees: a connected graph with $N$ vertices and $N-1$ edges is necessarily a tree.\n\nFor example, consider the graph below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1284/Source/Images/84c92a6b0268a8cf320c2c4e60ba1c1e.png)\n\nThis undirected simple graph is connected and acyclic, so it is a tree. It has $4$ vertices and $3$ edges. However, adding any edge introduces a cycle, making it no longer a tree, as illustrated below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1284/Source/Images/2007dfb9ffe710beecf7e4cefdb95f1f.png)\n\nIndeed, the graph above is not a tree because it contains a cycle: $v_1-v_2-v_4-v_1$."] [17592186106924 1029 "A subgraph of a connected graph $G$ is called a **spanning tree** of $G$ if it is a tree that includes all the vertices of $G$. Alternatively, a spanning tree of a connected graph $G$ with $N$ vertices is a connected subgraph with $N$ vertices and $N-1$ edges. This follows from the fact that any connected graph with $N$ vertices and $N-1$ edges is necessarily a tree.\n\nSince any connected graph with $N$ vertices has spanning trees with exactly $N-1$ edges, all spanning trees of an unweighted graph are \"minimal,\" meaning they are equal in terms of their numbers of edges.\n\nFor example, consider the graph below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1284/Source/Images/c8da72e9d9d949c5e0428a0ec2a0a7f0.png)\n\n$$\n7\n$$\n\n$$\n6\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1284/Source/Images/1fbd15dd06eb2ecc549b787cec712e91.png)"] [17592186106925 1029 "A **minimum spanning tree** (**MST**) of a weighted graph is a spanning tree with the smallest possible total weight. Like any spanning tree, an MST of a weighted graph with $N$ vertices contains exactly $N-1$ edges.\n\nFor example, consider the graph below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1284/Source/Images/62be66154bd8aa6f23fef69390101205.png)\n\nRecall that a spanning tree of $G$ must be acyclic and must include all the vertices of $G$.\n\nSince the graph above has only one cycle, removing any one of the edges in the cycle makes the graph acyclic, forming a tree. This tree retains all vertices of the original graph, making it a spanning tree. We should remove the edge with the largest weight to ensure our spanning tree is minimal.\n\nThe cycle has $3$ edges with weights of $3, 5$, and $6$. By removing the edge with the greatest weight $(6)$, we obtain the minimum possible total weight:\n\n$$\n9+3+5 = 17\n$$\n\nTherefore, the remaining edges, highlighted below, form the (unique, in this case) minimum spanning tree of the original graph.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1284/Source/Images/64a5164b01395368eae02ee5eb62720d.png)"] [17592186106926 1029 "Suppose that the base of a solid is the region enclosed between the curve\n\n$$\ny=\\sqrt{9-x^2}\n$$\n\nand the $x$-axis from $x=0$ to $x=3$, as shown in the picture. Given that cross-sections perpendicular to the $x$-axis are equilateral triangles, how can we find the volume of the solid?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1276/Source/Images/e6373a74d7bd8eed739190d7892ddfcb.png)\n\nA sketch with a few cross-sections is shown in the picture below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1276/Source/Images/bf476f47590829bbc29c67feb74f2bd2.png)\n\nConsider the picture with only one cross-section that passes through $x \\in (0,3)$:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1276/Source/Images/5e0db743bd42b54a116a707069a9d29f.png)\n\nIf we project the triangular cross-section onto the $xy$-plane, we get the picture below:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1276/Source/Images/88517cfe27e1cfff2c4f1161ac55a68c.png)\n\nThen, the base of the cross-section is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na &= \\mid y|= \\sqrt{9 - x^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven a side $a$ of an equilateral triangle, we can show that the corresponding area is given by\n\n$$\nA = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{4} a^2\n$$\n\nSo, the expression for the area of the cross-section is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA(x) &= \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}a^{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}(\\sqrt{9 - x^{2}})^{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}(9 - x^{2})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now write the volume of the solid, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV &= ∫_{a}^{b}A(x)dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}∫_{0}^{3}(9 - x^{2})dx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the integral above gives the required volume."] [17592186106927 1029 "Now, suppose that the base of a solid is the region enclosed between the curve\n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt{2-y^2}\n$$\n\nand the $y$-axis for\n\n$$\n0 \\leq y \\leq \\sqrt{2}\n$$\n\nas shown in the picture. If cross-sections perpendicular to the $y$-axis are isosceles right triangles with a leg lying on the $xy$-plane, can we find an integral that gives the volume of the solid?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1276/Source/Images/5122440079eca62133cb1eab8bbddef4.png)\n\nA sketch with a few cross-sections is shown in the picture below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1276/Source/Images/ce86d62e3caf63173b3f33bae2aefc3f.png)\n\nConsider the picture with only one cross-section that passes through $y \\in (0,\\sqrt{2})$:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1276/Source/Images/cc38229dac881b3336904988cb93dd73.png)\n\nIf we project the triangular cross-section onto the $xy$-plane, we get the picture below:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1276/Source/Images/a87ec64e181ab1ee58d5f1953b9098f9.png)\n\nThen, the base of the cross-section is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nb &= \\mid x|= \\sqrt{2 - y^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven a leg $b$ of an isosceles right triangle, we can find the corresponding area using the formula\n\n$$\nA = \\dfrac{b^2}{2}\n$$\n\nSo, the expression for the area of the cross-section is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA(y) &= \\frac{1}{2}b^{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}(\\sqrt{2 - y^{2}})^{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}(2 - y^{2})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now write the volume of the solid as an integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV &= ∫_{c}^{d}A(y)dy \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}∫_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}(2 - y^{2})dy\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the integral above gives the required volume."] [17592186106928 1029 "Suppose that the base of a solid is the region enclosed by the line\n\n$$\ny=-2x+2\n$$\n\nand the $x$-axis for\n\n$$\n0 \\leq x \\leq 1\n$$\n\nas shown below. If cross-sections perpendicular to the $x$-axis are semicircles, how do we calculate the volume of the solid?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1280/Source/Images/036b037bf71f038c34597060433516cc.png)\n\nA sketch with a few cross-sections is shown in the picture below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1280/Source/Images/46bd3268b31bdb0d428f3cb5cf115040.png)\n\nConsider a cross-section at some point $x$, where $x \\in (0,1)$, and its projection onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1280/Source/Images/a0d0d5d3ed9d43177bf3c6c7d1709f5b.png)\n\nIf we project the rectangular cross-section onto the $xy$-plane, we get the picture below:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1280/Source/Images/a9eac2941dbed8b9dc13141037b78ad8.png)\n\nThen, the base of the cross-section is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nd &= \\begin{vmatrix}y|= & -2x + 2\\end{vmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven the diameter $d$ of a semicircle, we can find the corresponding area using the formula\n\n$$\nA = \\overbrace{\\dfrac{1}{2} \\cdot \\underbrace{\\dfrac{1}{4}\\pi d^2}_{\\textrm{Full circle}}}^{\\textrm{Semicircle}} = \\dfrac{1}{8}\\pi d^2\n$$\n\nSo, the expression for the area of the cross-section is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA(x) &= \\frac{1}{8}πd^{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{π}{8} \\mid - 2x + 2 \\mid ^{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{π}{8}(4x^{2} - 8x + 4) \\\\\n&= \\frac{π}{2}(1 - 2x + x^{2})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now calculate the volume of the solid, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV &= ∫_{a}^{b}A(x)dx \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}\\frac{π}{2}(1 - 2x + x^{2})dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{π}{2}∫_{0}^{1}(1 - 2x + x^{2})dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{π}{2}(x - x^{2} + \\frac{x^{3}}{3}) \\mid _{0}^{1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{π}{2}[(1 - 1 + \\frac{1}{3}) - 0] \\\\\n&= \\frac{π}{2} \\cdot \\frac{1}{3} \\\\\n&= \\frac{π}{6}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186106929 1029 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1354/Source/Images/97a46734b8d4d80157f1d593212a7941.png)\n\nConsider the parallelogram $ABCD$ above, where\n\n$$\nm\\angle A = 70^\\circ\n$$\n\nLet's find the measures of the other angles.\n\n- First, we know that opposite angles in a parallelogram are congruent. Therefore, $m\\angle C = 70^\\circ$.![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1354/Source/Images/51e66e81af368c76e625c64ef9293bd0.png)\n\n- Next, we know that consecutive angles in a parallelogram are supplementary. Therefore,\n$m∠B|= 180^{∘} - m∠A; = 180^{∘} - 70^{∘}; = 110^{∘}$.\nLet's add this to our diagram.![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1354/Source/Images/2ccabf62de50e73074a1efa852f7693f.png)\n\n- Finally, we apply the fact that opposite angles are congruent to conclude that $m\\angle D = 110^\\circ$.![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1354/Source/Images/7fb3ebe5549fecae91825f0cef2ca56c.png)"] [17592186106930 1029 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1354/Source/Images/1b3a96e6a05ebbcb66d5a7c0fa10df4b.png)\n\nGiven any parallelogram, we have the following property.\n\n> *The opposite angles of a parallelogram are congruent, whereas adjacent (or consecutive) angles are supplementary.*\n\nWe'll learn how to prove these properties in future lessons."] [17592186106931 1029 "Consider the parallelogram shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1354/Source/Images/00191dcaf9989d9effbc88214fefe144.png)\n\nWhat are the measures of the sides $\\overline{CD}$ and $\\overline{BC}$?\n\n- Since the sides $AB$ and $CD$ are opposite, we have $CD = AB = 3$.\n- Since the sides $AD$ and $BC$ are opposite, we have $BC = AD = 2$.\n\nLet's add these lengths to our diagram.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1354/Source/Images/f510f6cc434bad2586831c89e22ff428.png)\n\nLet's see some more examples."] [17592186106932 1029 "Consider the following diagram.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1406/Source/Images/942f6b554102998c0bc96243b22147b7.png)\n\nNote that the tangent $\\overleftrightarrow{PQ}$ at $B$ and the chord $\\overline{AB}$ form two angles:\n\n- The *acute* angle $\\angle ABQ$.\n- The *obtuse* angle $\\angle ABP$.\n\nAccording to the tangent-chord theorem, the measure of the *obtuse* angle $\\angle ABP$ equals half the measure of the *major* intercepted arc $\\overset{\\frown}{ADB}{:}$\n\n$$\nm\\angle ABP = \\dfrac12 m\\overset{\\frown}{ADB}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠ABP &= \\frac{1}{2}mADB^(⌢) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 280^{∘} \\\\\n&= 140^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's consider another example."] [17592186106933 1029 "Now, let's suppose that the base of a solid is the region below enclosed by the line\n\n$$\nx=y+2\n$$\n\nand the $y$-axis for\n\n$$\n-2 \\leq y \\leq 0\n$$\n\nGiven that cross-sections perpendicular to the $y$-axis are semicircles, how do we calculate the volume of the solid?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1280/Source/Images/1a162b343fb0b5d88f298eea189ad69e.png)\n\nA sketch with a few cross-sections is shown in the picture below:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1280/Source/Images/c4de65e03352ed6d7deba328d5e0580a.png)\n\nNow, consider the picture with only one cross-section that passes through some point $y \\in (-2,0)$:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1280/Source/Images/e1309d6727079050161f38e0c663b341.png)\n\nThe projection of the previous picture on the $xy$-plane is given below:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1280/Source/Images/5d86770eab2428f845ca10c99f4ed720.png)\n\nThen, the base of the cross-section is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nd &= \\begin{vmatrix}y|= & y + 2\\end{vmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven the diameter $d$ of a semicircle, we can find the corresponding area using the formula\n\n$$\nA = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\cdot \\dfrac{1}{4}\\pi d^2 = \\dfrac{1}{8}\\pi d^2\n$$\n\nSo, the expression for the area of the cross-section is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA(y) &= \\frac{1}{8}πd^{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{π}{8} \\mid y + 2 \\mid ^{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{π}{8}(y^{2} + 4y + 4)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now calculate the volume of the solid, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV &= ∫_{c}^{d}A(y)dy \\\\\n&= ∫_{-2}^{0}\\frac{π}{8}(y^{2} + 4y + 4)dy \\\\\n&= \\frac{π}{8}∫_{-2}^{0}(y^{2} + 4y + 4)dy \\\\\n&= \\frac{π}{8}(\\frac{y^{3}}{3} + 2y^{2} + 4y) \\mid _{-2}^{0} \\\\\n&= \\frac{π}{8}[0 - (-\\frac{8}{3} + 8 - 8)] \\\\\n&= \\frac{π}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186106934 1029 "Consider the following diagram.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1357/Source/Images/9d31e5fae82ea046290c5d515d2f4668.png)\n\nSuppose we wish to formally prove the following congruence statement:\n\n> $\\triangle{ABD} \\cong \\triangle{ACD}$\n\nIn this instance, we can prove this statement using the SSS congruence criterion.\n\nWe proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1**: We're given that the segments $\\overline{AB}$ and $\\overline{AC}$ are congruent.\n$\\overline{AB} \\cong \\overline{AC}$\nLet's highlight this on our diagram.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1357/Source/Images/c302bd7366edac9f9e058f67ae25f527.png)\n\n- **Step 2**: We are also given that the segments $\\overline{BD}$ and $\\overline{CD}$ are congruent.\n$\\overline{BD} \\cong \\overline{CD}$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1357/Source/Images/fd18e725f39cb472c3ca4d9c93bd5af4.png)\n\n- **Step 3**: By the *reflexive* property of congruence, $\\overline{AD}$ is congruent to itself.\n$\\overline{AD} \\cong \\overline{AD}$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1357/Source/Images/3d0db48e71c9aaba8066b9672ff2aed6.png)\n\n- **Step 4**: From steps 1 and 2, we have\n$\\overline{AB} \\cong \\overline{AC}, \\qquad \\overline{BD} \\cong \\overline{CD}$.\nAlso, the segment $\\overline{AD}$ is a common side of both triangles and $\\overline{AD}\\cong \\overline{AD}$ from step 3. So, we have three pairs of congruent sides. Therefore, by the SSS criterion, we conclude that\n$\\triangle{ABD} \\cong \\triangle{ACD}$\nas required."] [17592186106935 1029 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1357/Source/Images/be8e397f62ee642fbf749a99cf23baa2.png)\n\nThe following table shows the proof that $\\triangle{ABD} \\cong \\triangle{ACD}$ in a two-column format.\n\n|  | Statement | Reason |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| 1 | $\\overline{AB} \\cong \\overline{AC}$ | Given |\n| 2 | $\\overline{BD} \\cong \\overline{CD}$ | Given |\n| 3 | $\\overline{AD} \\cong \\overline{AD}$ | Reflexive property of congruence |\n| 4 | $\\triangle{ABD} \\cong \\triangle{ACD}$ | SSS congruence criterion |"] [17592186106936 1029 "In this lesson, we will learn how to prove general statements about parallelograms. First, let's recall the definition of a parallelogram.\n\n> *A parallelogram is a quadrilateral where the opposite sides are parallel.*\n\nNext, recall the following property of parallelograms:\n\n> *In a parallelogram, the opposite sides are congruent.*\n\nTo prove this statement, consider the parallelogram shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1398/Source/Images/fb68883059d381b34da97dac49b1aa08.png)\n\nLet's prove the following statement:\n\n> $\\overline{MN} \\cong \\overline{PQ}$\n\nOur strategy is to show that a diagonal splits the parallelogram into two congruent triangles and then use the properties of congruent triangles (CPCTC) to prove the desired result.\n\nWe proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1**: We are *given* that $MNPQ$ is a parallelogram.\n\n- **Step 2**: By the *definition of a parallelogram*, $\\overline{MN} \\parallel \\overline{PQ}$ and $\\overline{MQ} \\parallel \\overline{NP}$. Let's add this information to our diagram.![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1398/Source/Images/658d48668861aafcb4bc8f06e589fb20.png)\n- **Step 3**: Now, let's construct the diagonal $\\overline{QN}$.![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1398/Source/Images/7baa99c606805d637451a96bd408448d.png)\nThe transversal (diagonal) $\\overline{QN}$ crosses the parallel segments $\\overline{MN}$ and $\\overline{PQ}$, forming alternate interior angles $\\angle{MNQ}$ and $\\angle{PQN}$.\nAlso $\\overline{QN}$ crosses the parallel segments $\\overline{MQ}$ and $\\overline{NP}$, forming alternate interior angles $\\angle{MQN}$ and $\\angle{PNQ}$.\nTherefore, by the *alternate interior angles theorem*,\n$\\angle{MNQ} \\cong \\angle{PQN}, \\qquad \\angle{MQN} \\cong \\angle{PNQ}$,\nas shown.![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1398/Source/Images/b9cf4686bae02582adae3eba8364e284.png)\n- **Step 4**: The *reflexive property of congruence* of segments states that every segment is congruent to itself. Therefore, $\\overline{QN} \\cong \\overline{QN}$.\n\n- **Step 5**: In steps 3 and 4, we showed that $\\triangle{QMN}$ and $\\triangle{NPQ}$ have a common side $\\overline{QN}$ and two pairs of congruent angles adjacent to it. Therefore,\n$\\triangle{QMN} \\cong \\triangle{NPQ}$\nby the *ASA criterion*.\n- **Step 6**: Since corresponding parts of congruent triangles are congruent (*CPCTC*), we conclude that\n$\\overline{MN} \\cong \\overline{PQ}$,\nas required.\n\n**Note**: CPCTC can be used to show that $\\overline{MQ} \\cong \\overline{NP}$, thus proving that *both* sets of opposite sides are parallel."] [17592186106937 1029 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1398/Source/Images/ded67fe12aedc312d8730c1e5c840b7a.png)\n\nThe table below shows the proof that opposite sides of a parallelogram are congruent in a two-column format.\n\n|  | Statement | Reason |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| 1 | $MNPQ$ is a parallelogram | Given |\n| 2 | $\\overline{MN} \\parallel \\overline{PQ}, \\: \\overline{MQ} \\parallel \\overline{NP}$ | Definition of a parallelogram |\n| 3 | $\\angle{MNQ} \\cong \\angle{NQP}, \\: \\angle{MQN} \\cong \\angle{QNP}$ | Alternate interior angles theorem |\n| 4 | $\\overline{QN} \\cong \\overline{QN}$ | Reflexive property of congruence |\n| 5 | $\\triangle{QMN} \\cong \\triangle{NPQ}$ | ASA criterion |\n| 6 | $\\overline{MN} \\cong \\overline{PQ}$ | CPCTC |"] [17592186106938 1029 "Let's remind ourselves of another important property of parallelograms.\n\n> *The diagonals of a parallelogram bisect each other.*\n\nTo prove this theorem, consider the following parallelogram. Note that the diagonals are already constructed.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1398/Source/Images/2066de1b36dd6debf40041a0df5e492f.png)\n\nWe wish to prove the following statement:\n\n> *$VZ = XZ$ and $WZ = YZ$.*\n\nWe can complete the proof of this theorem using congruent triangles, as follows:\n\n- **Step 1**: We are *given* that $VWXY$ is a parallelogram.\n- **Step 2**: By the *definition of a parallelogram*, $\\overline{VW} \\parallel \\overline{XY}$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1398/Source/Images/82261a0ab64dd1f3e38428cd20181add.png)\n\n- **Step 3**: The transversal $\\overline{WY}$ crosses the parallel segments $\\overline{VW}$ and $\\overline{XY}$, forming the pair of alternate interior angles. Therefore, by the *alternate interior angles theorem*,\n$\\angle{VWZ}\\cong\\angle{XYZ}$,\nas shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1398/Source/Images/4d942d817574ed37996cb6ab4e70dcda.png)\n\n- **Step 4**: Note that $\\angle{VZW}$ and $\\angle{XZY}$ are vertical angles. Therefore, by the *vertical angles theorem*,\n$\\angle{VZW}\\cong\\angle{XZY}$,\nas shown.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1398/Source/Images/bc640f10ca1e47b1630551786937c1e1.png)\n\n- **Step 5**: Since $VWXY$ is a parallelogram, its opposite sides are congruent by the *properties of parallelograms.* Therefore,\n$\\overline{VW} \\cong \\overline{XY}$,\nas shown.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1398/Source/Images/78b7f52e049010c646539a1ff0817f81.png)\n\n- **Step 6**: From steps 3, 4, and 5, we have\n$\\angle{VWZ} \\cong \\angle{XYZ}, \\qquad \\angle{VZW} \\cong \\angle{XZY}, \\qquad \\overline{VW} \\cong \\overline{XY}$.\nTherefore,\n$\\triangle{VWZ} \\cong \\triangle{XYZ}$\nby the *AAS criterion* for triangle congruence.\n- **Step 7**: Since corresponding parts of congruent triangles are congruent (*CPCTC*), we have\n$\\overline{VZ} \\cong \\overline{XZ}, \\qquad \\overline{WZ} \\cong \\overline{YZ}$.\n- **Step 8**: By the *definition of congruent segments*, we conclude that\n$VZ = XZ, \\qquad WZ = YZ$,\nas required."] [17592186106939 1029 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1398/Source/Images/c5a9e6a3f34fe5d57563c5c825c7bba9.png)\n\nThe table below shows the proof that the diagonals of a parallelogram bisect each other in a two-column format.\n\n|  | Statement | Reason |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| 1 | $VWXY$ is a parallelogram | Given |\n| 2 | $\\overline{VW} \\parallel \\overline{XY}$ | Definition of a parallelogram |\n| 3 | $\\angle{VWZ} \\cong \\angle{XYZ}$ | Alternate interior angles theorem |\n| 4 | $\\angle{VZW} \\cong \\angle{XZY}$ | Vertical angles theorem |\n| 5 | $\\overline{VW} \\cong \\overline{XY}$ | Property of a parallelogram |\n| 6 | $\\triangle{VWZ} \\cong \\triangle{XYZ}$ | AAS criterion |\n| 7 | $\\overline{VZ} \\cong \\overline{XZ}$ and $\\overline{WZ} \\cong \\overline{YZ}$ | CPCTC |\n| 8 | $VZ=XZ$ and $WZ=YZ$ | Definition of congruent segments |\n\nNext, we'll consider a proof of the converse theorem:\n\n> *If the diagonals of a quadrilateral bisect each other, then it is a parallelogram.*"] [17592186106940 1029 "In this lesson, we'll learn how to prove statements concerning congruent triangles.\n\nBefore we start, let's remind ourselves of some definitions. These will be useful for the proofs that follow:\n\n- Congruent segments:*Two segments are congruent if they have the same length.*\n- Angle bisector:*An angle bisector is a line that divides an angle into two congruent angles.*\n- Midpoint:*The midpoint of a line segment is the point that splits it into two segments of equal length.*\n- Equilateral triangle:*A triangle is equilateral if all three sides are congruent.*\n- Isosceles triangle:*A triangle is isosceles if two (and only two) sides are congruent.*\n- Right triangle:*A triangle is right if one of its internal angles is a right angle.*\n- Median:*A median of a triangle is a line segment joining a vertex to the midpoint of the opposite side.*\n- Altitude (or Height):*An altitude (height) of a triangle is a line segment drawn from one vertex of the triangle perpendicular to the opposite side (or the extension of the opposite side).*\n- Perpendicular lines:*Two lines are perpendicular if they form a right angle at their intersection point.*\n\nWe'll also make use of the following:\n\n- The right angle postulate:*All right angles are congruent.*\n- The SSS, SAS, AAS, ASA, and HL congruence criteria for triangles.\n- The reflexive, symmetric, and transitive properties of equality and congruence."] [17592186106941 1029 "We can use a similar approach to prove the following property:\n\n> *The opposite angles of a parallelogram are congruent.*\n\nWe also have the following.\n\n> *The adjacent (or consecutive) angles of a parallelogram are supplementary.*\n\nLet's see a proof of the consecutive angles property."] [17592186106942 1029 "**Kruskal's algorithm** finds a *minimum spanning tree* of a connected, weighted graph $G$. It follows a so-called **greedy approach,** selecting the edges in increasing order of weight while avoiding cycles. It is used in network design, cluster analysis in machine learning, and image segmentation.\n\nThe steps for the Kruskal's algorithm are as follows:\n\n- **Step 1:** Initiate a forest containing all the vertices of $G$ but no edges.\n- **Step 2:** Order the edges of $G$ by their weight from smallest to largest.\n- **Step 3:** Iterate through this list of edges in ascending order, adding an edge to the forest only if its ends belong to different connected components (i.e., only if it doesn't create a cycle).\n- **Step 4:** Terminate the iteration when all vertices are included in a single connected component with no cycles.\n\nWe can express Kruskal's algorithm using **pseudocode**. Pseudocode is a simplified, programming language-independent way of describing an algorithm. It uses a mix of natural language and programming concepts, helping programmers to plan and visualize code logic without focusing on syntax.\n\nThe pseudocode for Kruskal's algorithm is as follows.\n\n> `Initialize a forest containing all the vertices of G and none\n> of its edges`\n> `def Kruskal(): # Kruskal's algorithm`\n> `Order the edges of G by their weight from smallest to largest`\n> `for (e in ordered list of edges): # Go over all edges`\n> `if (ends of e belong to different connected components):`\n> `Add e to the forest`\n\nLet's take a look at how to apply Kruskal's algorithm to a concrete graph."] [17592186106943 1029 "**Prim's algorithm** finds a minimum spanning tree of a connected, weighted graph $G$. It follows a so-called **greedy** approach by starting from an arbitrary vertex and expanding the tree by adding the smallest edge that connects a visited vertex to an unvisited vertex. It is widely used in network design, constructing road systems, and optimizing cable connections.\n\nThe steps for Prim's algorithm are as follows:\n\n- **Step 1:** Initialize a tree $T$ consisting of a starting vertex $v$ of $G$.\n- **Step 2:** While not all vertices are in $T$, find the shortest edge $u - w$ such that $u$ is already in $T$ and $w$ is not.\n- **Step 3:** Add vertex $w$ and edge $u - w$ to tree $T$.\n- **Step 4:** Repeat this process until all vertices are included in $T$.\n\nWe can express Prim's algorithm using **pseudocode.** Pseudocode is a simplified, programming language-independent way of describing an algorithm. It uses a mix of natural language and programming concepts, helping programmers to plan and visualize code logic without focusing on syntax.\n\nThe pseudocode for Prim's algorithm is as follows.\n\n> `Initialize a tree T consisting of a starting vertex v of G`\n> `while (not all vertices added to T):`\n> `Find the shortest edge u-w of G such that\n> (u in T) and (w not in T)`\n> `Add vertex w and edge u-w to tree T`\n\nLet's take a look at how to apply Prim's algorithm to a concrete graph."] [17592186106944 1029 "Suppose we wish to find the number of *distinct* permutations of the following letters:\n$a, \\qquad a, \\qquad b, \\qquad b, \\qquad c$\nThere are two $a$'s, two $b$'s, and one $c$, and a total of $5$ letters.\n\nFirst, recall that there are $5$! ways of arranging a sequence of $5$ objects. However, some arrangements will be the same since we have repeated letters. For example, we get the same final arrangement by swapping the first and the second positions in the sequence above.\n\nIf we were to list all arrangements of the above sequence by shuffling only the repeated letters among themselves (using suffixes to track the repeated letters), we get the following:\n\n$$\\begin{array}{r}{a}_{1} , \\, {a}_{2} , \\, {b}_{1} , \\, {b}_{2} , \\, c \\\\ {a}_{2} , \\, {a}_{1} , \\, {b}_{1} , \\, {b}_{2} , \\, c \\\\ {a}_{1} , \\, {a}_{2} , \\, {b}_{2} , \\, {b}_{1} , \\, c \\\\ {a}_{2} , \\, {a}_{1} , \\, {b}_{2} , \\, {b}_{1} , \\, c\\end{array}$$\n\nRearranging the $a$'s among themselves in any way will not change the final result, nor will rearranging the $b$'s and $c$'s.\n\nNotice that we have $4$ ways of rearranging the letters among themselves:\n\n- Since we have two $a$'s, there are $2$! ways of arranging them.\n- Since we have two $b$'s, there are $2$! ways of arranging them.\n- Since we have one $c$, there is only $1!=1$ way of arranging it.\n\nIn total, there are\n\n$$\n2! \\cdot 2! \\cdot 1! = 4\n$$\n\nways of arranging letters of our sequence if we can shuffle only the same letters among themselves.\n\nSo, to find the number of *distinct* permutations of our sequence, we need to divide the total number of arrangements $(5!)$ by the number of arrangements that do not change anything ($2! \\cdot 2! \\cdot 1$!):\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5!}{2! \\cdot 2! \\cdot 1!} = 30\n$$\n\nTherefore, there are $30$ distinct ways of arranging this sequence of letters."] [17592186106945 1029 "Recall that a cycle in a graph is a walk with no repeated vertices, except for the first and last, which are the same.\n\nA **directed acyclic graph** (**DAG**) is a directed graph that contains no directed cycles.\n\nAn example of a DAG is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1296/Source/Images/e53d6225a8dd55c3f1c4e26d91f63c41.png)"] [17592186106946 1029 "The same graph that visually appears to have cycles when treated as undirected may or may not be acyclic when directed.\n\nAmong the graphs below:\n\n- $G_1$ is an undirected cyclic graph with a cycle $v_1 - v_2 - v_3 - v_4 - v_1$.\n- $G_2$ is a directed cyclic graph with a cycle $v_1 \\to v_2 \\to v_3 \\to v_4 \\to v_1$.\n- $G_3$ is a directed acyclic graph with no cycles.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1296/Source/Images/aac3e149088207ba51c7516d76cd3568.png)"] [17592186106947 1029 "The **transitive closure** of a directed acyclic graph (DAG) is a graph where every possible edge is added while preserving the same reachability relation between vertices as in the original DAG. The transitive closure of a DAG is uniquely determined.\n\nThe algorithm for building the transitive closure is straightforward: for every pair of vertices $v$ and $u$ such that $u$ is reachable from $v$ indirectly through a sequence of edges, add a direct edge $v \\to u$.\n\nFor example, consider the directed acyclic graph below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1296/Source/Images/bf0a67ba01c15e7b5190624d2dd997ba.png)\n\nTo build the transitive closure, we add a directed edge for every pair of vertices $v$ and $u$ where $u$ is reachable from $v$ indirectly, as follows:\n\n- $v_4$ is reachable from $v_1$ through the path $v_1 \\to v_6 \\to v_5 \\to v_4$; hence, we add a new edge $v_1 \\to v_4$.\n- $v_4$ is reachable from $v_6$ through the path $v_6 \\to v_5 \\to v_4$; hence, we add a new edge $v_6 \\to v_4$.\n- No other edges can be added while preserving the same reachability relation.\n\nThe original DAG and its transitive closure are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1296/Source/Images/5efbfc44e6d8fc9167e7f88a4205d90d.png)\n\nThe **transitive reduction** of a directed acyclic graph (DAG) is a graph where all unnecessary edges are removed while preserving the same reachability relation between vertices as in the original DAG. The transitive reduction of a DAG is uniquely determined.\n\nThe algorithm for building the transitive reduction is straightforward: attempt to remove each existing edge $e$ and check whether *all* the reachability relations of the original DAG are preserved.\n\nFor example, consider the directed acyclic graph below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1296/Source/Images/c705cf7a848f205f6b08609b78da2376.png)\n\nTo build the transitive reduction, we attempt to remove each existing edge while ensuring that all reachability relations of the original DAG are preserved, as follows:\n\n- The edge $v_1 \\to v_4$ can be removed because $v_4$ remains reachable from $v_1$ through the path $v_1 \\to v_5 \\to v_4$.\n- The edge $v_1 \\to v_5$ can be removed because $v_5$ remains reachable from $v_1$ through the path $v_1 \\to v_6 \\to v_5$.\n- The edge $v_6 \\to v_4$ can also be removed because $v_4$ remains reachable from $v_6$ through the path $v_6 \\to v_5 \\to v_4$.\n- No additional edges can be removed while preserving the same reachability relation.\n\nThe original DAG and its transitive reduction are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1296/Source/Images/b0a941192d218269b1d2e3826b5db306.png)\n\nNotice that if we first build the transitive closure of a DAG and then apply the transitive reduction to this closure, we do not generally obtain the original DAG. The two examples above illustrate this point."] [17592186106948 1029 "For a directed acyclic graph (DAG), the **reachability relation** between two vertices is defined as follows:\n\n> *A vertex $u$ is **reachable** from a vertex $v$ if there exists a directed path from $v$ to $u$.*\n\nTwo properties of the reachability relation for directed acyclic graphs are:\n\n- *Transitivity*: if $u$ is reachable from $v$ and $w$ is reachable from $u$, then $w$ is reachable from $v$.\n- *Antisymmetry*: If $u$ is reachable from $v$ and $v$ is reachable from $u$ then $u = v$.\n\nNotice that the reachability relation is transitive and *symmetric* (if $u$ is reachable from $v$, then $v$ is reachable from $u$) for undirected graphs because undirected paths can be reversed.\n\nThe vertices reachable from $v$ are called the **successors** of $v$. The set of all successors of a vertex $v$ in a DAG is called the **successor set** of $v$. The following image highlights the successor set of vertex $v_1$ along with the edges leading to those successors.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1296/Source/Images/b4909a40757f029d0c22d2267b02f2c5.png)\n\nThus, the successor set of $v_1$ is $\\{v_2, v_4, v_5\\}$.\n\nThe vertices from which it is possible to reach $u$ are called the **predecessors** of $u$. The set of all predecessors of a vertex $u$ in a DAG is called the **predecessor set** of $u$.\n\nThe following image highlights the predecessor set of vertex $v_5$, along with the edges leading to that set.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1296/Source/Images/3fd71f1cbe3c943eaab802fa33f15648.png)\n\nThus, the predecessor set of $v_5$ is $\\{v_1, v_2, v_3\\}$."] [17592186106949 1029 "The area bounded by a polar curve\n\n$$\nr = r(\\theta)\n$$\n\nand the rays\n\n$$\n\\theta = \\theta_1\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\theta = \\theta_2\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 1 2\\int_{\\theta_1}^{\\theta_2} r^2\\,\\textrm d \\theta \\,\n$$\n\nCalculating the integral is often straightforward if we know the integration limits $\\theta_1$ and $\\theta_2$. But what if we don't know these values?\n\nFor example, suppose we want to calculate the total area of the first petal of the curve\n\n$$\nr=\\sin(3\\theta)\n$$\n\nshown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1345/Source/Images/51bb7490f42a6a4fd4cdb7b162aa88c0.png)\n\nThe curve bounding the blue petal crosses itself at the origin. When it passes through the origin, we must have $r=0$, and since\n\n$$\nr=\\sin(3\\theta)\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\sin(3\\theta)=0 \\,\n$$\n\nThis gives us two solutions for $[MATH: 3\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi):]$\n\n$$\n3\\theta = 0,\\pi \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\theta=0,\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta_1 = 0, \\theta_2 = \\dfrac\\pi 3\n$$\n\nand the area of the first petal is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= \\frac{1}{2}∫_{0}^{π/3}\\sin^{2} (3θ)dθ \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{4}∫_{0}^{π/3}(1 - \\cos (6θ))dθ \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{4}(θ \\mid _{0}^{π/3} - \\frac{1}{6}\\sin (6θ) \\mid _{0}^{π/3}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{4}(\\frac{π}{3} + 0) \\\\\n&= \\frac{π}{12}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186106950 1029 "Recall that the distance between vertices $u$ and $v$ in an unweighted directed graph, $d(u,v)$, is the length (i.e., the number of edges) of the shortest path from $u$ to $v$. If no path from $u$ to $v$ exists, we say that the distance is $\\infty$.\n\nIn a simple directed unweighted graph $G=(V,E)$ with $n$ vertices $v_1, v_2, \\ldots, v_n$, the **distance matrix** $D$ of the graph is an $n \\times n$ matrix, where the entry $a_{ij}$ at the intersection of the $i$th row and the $j$th column represents the *distance* between the vertices $v_i$ and $v_j$.\n\nNotice that, according to this definition:\n\n- If no path exists between $v_i$ and $v_j$, then the entry $a_{ij}$ is $\\infty$.\n- If $i=j$ (i.e., it is the same vertex), then the entry $a_{ii}$ is $0$.\n- The distance matrix of a directed graph is *not* symmetric about the main diagonal in general.\n\nLet's look at an example of how to build the distance matrix for the graph below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1392/Source/Images/e1067a2ada4c6ba237654e8737c43670.png)\n\nWe start with a square $4 \\times 4$ matrix with $0$s on the main diagonal and examine every pair of vertices to determine the remaining matrix entries.\n\n- The path $v_1 \\to v_2 \\to v_3 \\to v_4$ has three edges and is the shortest path between $v_1$ and $v_4$. Hence, we write $3$ in the entry $a_{14}$.\n\n|  | $v_1$ | $v_2$ | $v_3$ | $v_4$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $v_1$ | $0$ |  |  | $3$ |\n| $v_2$ |  | $0$ |  |  |\n| $v_3$ |  |  | $0$ |  |\n| $v_4$ |  |  |  | $0$ |\n\n- Similarly, the path $v_2 \\to v_3 \\to v_4$ has two edges and is the shortest path between $v_2$ and $v_4$. Hence, we write $2$ in the entry $a_{24}$.\n\n|  | $v_1$ | $v_2$ | $v_3$ | $v_4$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $v_1$ | $0$ |  |  | $3$ |\n| $v_2$ |  | $0$ |  | $2$ |\n| $v_3$ |  |  | $0$ |  |\n| $v_4$ |  |  |  | $0$ |\n\n- Similarly, the path $v_4 \\to v_2 \\to v_3$ has two edges and is the shortest path between $v_4$ and $v_3$. Hence, we write $2$ in the entry $a_{43}$.\n\n|  | $v_1$ | $v_2$ | $v_3$ | $v_4$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $v_1$ | $0$ |  |  | $3$ |\n| $v_2$ |  | $0$ |  | $2$ |\n| $v_3$ |  |  | $0$ |  |\n| $v_4$ |  |  | $2$ | $0$ |\n\n- We write $1$ in every entry that represents a vertex pair connected by a direct edge: $a_{12}, a_{23}, a_{34}$, and $a_{42}$.\n\n|  | $v_1$ | $v_2$ | $v_3$ | $v_4$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $v_1$ | $0$ | $1$ |  | $3$ |\n| $v_2$ |  | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| $v_3$ |  |  | $0$ | $1$ |\n| $v_4$ |  | $1$ | $2$ | $0$ |\n\n- Finally, we write $\\infty$ in all other empty cells to indicate that no paths exist between the corresponding vertex pairs.\n\n|  | $v_1$ | $v_2$ | $v_3$ | $v_4$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $v_1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $\\infty$ | $3$ |\n| $v_2$ | $\\infty$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| $v_3$ | $\\infty$ | $\\infty$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n| $v_4$ | $\\infty$ | $1$ | $2$ | $0$ |\n\nThe distance matrix is completed. In regular matrix notation, we can write this distance matrix as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & ∞ & 3 \\\\ ∞ & 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ ∞ & ∞ & 0 & 1 \\\\ ∞ & 1 & 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186106951 1029 "Recall that the distance between vertices $u$ and $v$ in a weighted graph, $d(u,v)$, is the length (i.e., sum of the weights of edges) of the shortest path from $u$ to $v$. If no path from $u$ to $v$ exists, we say that the distance is $\\infty$.\n\nIn a weighted graph $G=(V,E)$ with $n$ vertices $v_1, v_2, \\ldots, v_n$, the **distance matrix** $D$ of the graph is an $n \\times n$ matrix, where the entry $a_{ij}$ at the intersection of the $i$th row and the $j$th column equals the *distance* between the vertices $v_i$ and $v_j$. Here $G$ can be either directed or undirected.\n\nNotice that, according to this definition:\n\n- If no path exists between $v_i$ and $v_j$, then the entry $a_{ij}$ is $\\infty$.\n- If $i=j$ (i.e., it is the same vertex), then the entry $a_{ii}$ is $0$.\n- The distance matrix of an undirected weighted graph is symmetric about the main diagonal, but the distance matrix of a directed weighted graph is *not* symmetric in general.\n\nLet's look at an example of how to build the distance matrix for the directed weighted graph below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1392/Source/Images/e7743d89335b75e3a757b09cb9f5e69a.png)\n\nWe start with a square $4 \\times 4$ matrix with $0$s on the main diagonal and examine every pair of vertices to determine the remaining matrix entries.\n\n- The detour path $v_1 \\to v_2 \\to v_3 \\to v_4$ has a weight of $2+1+5=8$ and is the shortest path between $v_1$ and $v_4$. (Notice that the alternative path $v_1 \\to v_2 \\to v_4$, which has fewer edges, has a greater weight of $2+7=9$.) Hence, we write $8$ in the entry $a_{14}$.\n\n|  | $v_1$ | $v_2$ | $v_3$ | $v_4$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $v_1$ | $0$ |  |  | $8$ |\n| $v_2$ |  | $0$ |  |  |\n| $v_3$ |  |  | $0$ |  |\n| $v_4$ |  |  |  | $0$ |\n\n- Similarly, the path $v_2 \\to v_3 \\to v_4$ has a weight of $1+5=6$ and is the shortest path between $v_2$ and $v_4$. Hence, we write $6$ in the entry $a_{24}$.\n\n|  | $v_1$ | $v_2$ | $v_3$ | $v_4$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $v_1$ | $0$ |  |  | $8$ |\n| $v_2$ |  | $0$ |  | $6$ |\n| $v_3$ |  |  | $0$ |  |\n| $v_4$ |  |  |  | $0$ |\n\n- We write the weight of an edge in the entry corresponding to a vertex pair connected by a direct edge with no shorter detours exist: $a_{12}, a_{23}$, and $a_{34}$.\n\n|  | $v_1$ | $v_2$ | $v_3$ | $v_4$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $v_1$ | $0$ | $2$ |  | $8$ |\n| $v_2$ |  | $0$ | $1$ | $6$ |\n| $v_3$ |  |  | $0$ | $5$ |\n| $v_4$ |  |  |  | $0$ |\n\n- Finally, we write $\\infty$ in all other empty cells to indicate that no paths exist between the corresponding vertex pairs.\n\n|  | $v_1$ | $v_2$ | $v_3$ | $v_4$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $v_1$ | $0$ | $2$ | $\\infty$ | $8$ |\n| $v_2$ | $\\infty$ | $0$ | $1$ | $6$ |\n| $v_3$ | $\\infty$ | $\\infty$ | $0$ | $5$ |\n| $v_4$ | $\\infty$ | $\\infty$ | $\\infty$ | $0$ |\n\nThe distance matrix is completed. In regular matrix notation, we can write this distance matrix as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 & ∞ & 8 \\\\ ∞ & 0 & 1 & 6 \\\\ ∞ & ∞ & 0 & 5 \\\\ ∞ & ∞ & ∞ & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186106952 1029 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1393/Source/Images/92402ae32dc0f3e1070d0b97b33a60a0.png)\n\nLet's use Kruskal's algorithm to find a minimum spanning tree of the graph given above.\n\nFirst, we put all the vertices of our graph into a forest with no edges.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1393/Source/Images/a943b915ac4e05ad3f68abd6612138e2.png)\n\nThe dashed lines denote the edges of the original graph.\n\nNext, we order the edges of the original graph according to their weights:\n\n$$\nv_{1} - v_{4}, v_{1} - v_{5}, v_{4} - v_{5}, v_{3} - v_{5}, v_{3} - v_{4},; v_{2} - v_{3}, v_{1} - v_{2}, v_{2} - v_{5}\n$$\n\nNow, we consider each edge in turn.\n\n- If an edge's endpoints (vertices) are in two *different* connected components of the forest, we add the edge to the forest.\n- If an edge's endpoints (vertices) are in the *same* connected components of the forest, we do *not* add the edge to the forest.\n\nWe proceed step-by-step.\n\n| **Step 1.** |  |\n| --- | ---: |\n| ![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1393/Source/Images/4cf6cbc7df6eb7816253ee6f99cf0e1f.png) | Add the edge $v_1 - v_4$ of the smallest weight to the forest. |\n| **Step 2.** |  |\n| ![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1393/Source/Images/38f43f8cf53f2928996ab1eb3ec97915.png) | Add the edge $v_1 - v_5$ of the smallest weight to the forest. |\n| **Step 3.** |  |\n| ![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1393/Source/Images/7e0c9b9aad03703fd84de95d16e3d468.png) | The next edge in the list is $v_4 - v_5$. Since its ends belong to the same connected component of the forest, we don't add it to the forest (adding it to the forest would form a cycle). |\n| **Step 4.** |  |\n| ![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1393/Source/Images/e544bac829d8298749ad5150bf99a36b.png) | Add the edge $v_3 - v_5$ of the smallest weight to the forest. |\n| **Step 5.** |  |\n| ![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1393/Source/Images/c2005f13a0016d8e17b1c4d3bf677733.png) | The next edge in the list is $v_3 - v_4$. Since its ends belong to the same connected component of the forest, we don't add it to the forest (adding it to the forest would form a cycle). |\n| **Step 6.** |  |\n| ![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1393/Source/Images/ca2f381c20c5e73400dd25a6109e516b.png) | Add the edge $v_2 - v_3$ of the smallest weight to the forest. |\n\nAll vertices of the initial graph are included in a single connected component with no cycles. So, we obtained a spanning tree.\n\nThe total weight of the resulting minimum spanning tree is\n\n$$\n4 + 5 + 7 + 11 = 27\n$$"] [17592186106953 1029 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1402/Source/Images/19ce7982530cab9bea775b5a46aa379a.png)\n\nLet's use Prim's algorithm, starting from the vertex $v_1$, to find a minimum spanning tree of the above graph.\n\nWe proceed as follows:\n\n| **Step 1.** |  |\n| --- | ---: |\n| ![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1402/Source/Images/c0a1c6ee4303e5dffffe077a6352b46b.png) | Add the vertex $v_1$ to the tree. |\n| **Step 2.** |  |\n| ![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1402/Source/Images/ad28ce10b58fc1d4237d8ff98e3e5ac5.png) | For the edge $v_1-v_3$, we have $v_1$ in the tree, $v_3$ not in the tree, and it's the shortest of the remaining edges with this property. So, we add vertex $v_3$ and edge to our tree. |\n| **Step 3.** |  |\n| ![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1402/Source/Images/f3727c9af20848de79a1a282c0bcabe8.png) | For the edge $v_3-v_4$, we have $v_3$ in the tree, $v_4$ not in the tree, and it's the shortest of the remaining edges with this property. So, we add vertex $v_4$ and edge to our tree. |\n| **Step 4.** |  |\n| ![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1402/Source/Images/5d2ce15df09e9be578289b866e195448.png) | For the edge $v_3-v_2$, we have $v_3$ in the tree, $v_2$ not in the tree, and it's the shortest of the remaining edges with this property. So, we add vertex $v_2$ and edge to our tree. |\n| **Step 5.** |  |\n| ![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1402/Source/Images/728b6b4f9276520a8ac8a762db0d5810.png) | For the edge $v_3-v_5$, we have $v_3$ in the tree, $v_5$ not in the tree, and it's the shortest of the remaining edges with this property. So, we add vertex $v_5$ and edge to our tree. |\n\nThe total weight of the resulting minimum spanning tree is\n\n$$\n40 + 33 + 36 + 38 = 147\n$$"] [17592186106954 1029 "Consider $\\triangle ABC$ with sides of lengths $a,b$, and $c$, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1275/Source/Images/85cb06d9cd067b372393114bddfa976d.png)\n\nLet's now add the height of length $h$ perpendicular to $\\overline{BC}$, as shown.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1275/Source/Images/5b6c83c07b7266920d24646cfb6f22af.png)\n\nUsing the usual formula for the area $\\mathcal A$ of a triangle in terms of its base and height, we have\n\n$$\n\\mathcal A = \\dfrac12 ah\n$$\n\nUsing elementary trigonometry, we have that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{h}{b} = \\sin C\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nh = b\\sin C\n$$\n\nTherefore, by substituting this into our expression for $\\mathcal A$, we have\n\n$$\n\\mathcal A = \\dfrac12ab\\sin C\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nWe can use a similar approach to prove the following formulas:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= \\frac{1}{2}bc\\sin A \\\\\nA &= \\frac{1}{2}ac\\sin B\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186106955 1029 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1270/Source/Images/e8e161cc73f98b78d480bfe29225c76d.png)\n\nSuppose we want to derive the polar equation of a circle with\n\n- center at $(a, 0)$, where $a \\ne 0$, and\n- radius $\\mid a \\mid$, so that it passes through the origin.\n\nLet a point $P$ on the circle have polar coordinates $(r, \\theta)$. Then, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are\n\n$$\n(x, y) = (r\\cos\\theta, \\, r\\sin\\theta)\n$$\n\nThe distance from $P$ to the center $(a, 0)$ is equal to the radius $\\mid a \\mid$. We can describe this using the distance formula:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{(r\\cos\\theta - a)^2 + (r\\sin\\theta)^2} = \\mid a \\mid\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides, we get\n\n$$\n(r\\cos\\theta - a)^2 + (r\\sin\\theta)^2 = a^2\n$$\n\nExpanding both terms yields\n\n$$\nr^2\\cos^2\\theta - 2ar\\cos\\theta + a^2 + r^2\\sin^2\\theta = a^2\n$$\n\nThen, we combine like terms:\n\n$$\nr^2(\\cos^2\\theta + \\sin^2\\theta) - 2ar\\cos\\theta + a^2 = a^2\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n\\cos^2\\theta + \\sin^2\\theta = 1\n$$\n\n, we can simplify the above as follows:\n\n$$\nr^2 - 2ar\\cos\\theta + a^2 = a^2\n$$\n\nSubtracting $a^2$ from both sides gives\n\n$$\nr^2 - 2ar\\cos\\theta = 0\n$$\n\nThen, we factor our equation:\n\n$$\nr(r - 2a\\cos\\theta) = 0\n$$\n\nSo, either $r = 0$ (which corresponds to the origin, already on the circle), or\n\n$$\nr = 2a\\cos\\theta\n$$\n\nwhich is the desired polar equation of the circle."] [17592186106956 1029 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1270/Source/Images/20230f2bf8ee224225026f59c7136416.png)\n\nSuppose we want to derive the polar equation of a circle with\n\n- center at $(0, a)$, where $a \\ne 0$, and\n- radius $\\mid a \\mid$, so that it passes through the origin.\n\nLet a point $P$ on the circle have polar coordinates $(r, \\theta)$. Then, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are\n\n$$\n(x, y) = (r\\cos\\theta, \\, r\\sin\\theta)\n$$\n\nThe distance from $P$ to the center $(0, a)$ is equal to the radius $\\mid a \\mid$. We can describe this using the distance formula:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{(r\\cos\\theta - 0)^2 + (r\\sin\\theta - a)^2} = \\mid a \\mid\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides, we get\n\n$$\n(r\\cos\\theta)^2 + (r\\sin\\theta - a)^2 = a^2\n$$\n\nExpanding both terms yields\n\n$$\nr^2\\cos^2\\theta + r^2\\sin^2\\theta - 2ar\\sin\\theta + a^2 = a^2\n$$\n\nThen, we combine like terms:\n\n$$\nr^2(\\cos^2\\theta + \\sin^2\\theta) - 2ar\\sin\\theta + a^2 = a^2\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n\\cos^2\\theta + \\sin^2\\theta = 1\n$$\n\n, we simplify:\n\n$$\nr^2 - 2ar\\sin\\theta + a^2 = a^2\n$$\n\nSubtracting $a^2$ from both sides gives\n\n$$\nr^2 - 2ar\\sin\\theta = 0\n$$\n\nThen, we factor our equation:\n\n$$\nr(r - 2a\\sin\\theta) = 0\n$$\n\nSo, either $r = 0$ (which corresponds to the origin, already on the circle), or\n\n$$\nr = 2a\\sin\\theta\n$$\n\nwhich is the desired polar equation of the circle."] [17592186106957 1029 "Multiplication of a power function by any *positive* real constant does not influence its limit at infinity. For example,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → ∞)2x^{2} &= lim_(x → ∞)x^{2} = ∞\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nbecause $y=2x^2$ is an upward parabola, just like $y=x^2$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1263/Source/Images/76185aa4a61b4811f227bc51cf486318.png)\n\nOn the other hand, multiplication of a power function by a *negative* real constant changes the *sign* of the limit. For example,\n\n$$\nlim_(x → ∞) - 2x^{2} =-lim_(x → ∞)x^{2} = - ∞\n$$\n\nbecause $y=-2x^2$ is a downwards parabola that points in the opposite direction as $y=x^2$."] [17592186106958 1029 "Rational functions have infinite limits when the $x$-value approaches vertical asymptotes. To find the infinite limits of a rational function $f(x)$, we first find the vertical asymptotes.\n\nFor example, consider the rational function\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{1}{x-2}\n$$\n\nThis rational function has a vertical asymptote at $x=2$.\n\nTo find the one-sided limits at the asymptote $x=2$, we check the sign of $f(x)$ as $x$ approaches $2$ from the left and the right.\n\n- To find the limit as $x \\to 2^-$, we evaluate $f(x)$ at $x$-values very close to $x=2$ from the left:\n\n| $x$ | $1.9$ | $1.99$ | $1.999$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $f(x)$ | $-10$ | $-100$ | $-1\\,000$ |\n\nThe values of $f(x)$ are negative, and they get more and more negative. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x \\to 2^-} f(x) = -\\infty\n$$\n\n- To find the limit as $x \\to 2^+$, we evaluate $f(x)$ at $x$-values very close to $x=2$ from the right:\n\n| $x$ | $2.1$ | $2.01$ | $2.001$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $f(x)$ | $10$ | $100$ | $1\\,000$ |\n\nThe values of $f(x)$ are positive, and they get larger and larger. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x \\to 2^+} f(x) = +\\infty\n$$\n\nThese one-sided limits match up with what we see in the graph of $f(x)$, shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1384/Source/Images/1f0de881aee9cf164ae6f91299a59145.png)"] [17592186106959 1029 "Two expressions involving fractions are **equivalent** if they are the same.\n\nEven if two expressions initially appear different, they might still be equivalent. For example, the expressions\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2}x\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x}{2}\n$$\n\nboth represent the same thing: \"half of $x$.\" Consequently, they are equivalent.\n\nWe can also see that these expressions are equivalent because\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2}x\n$$\n\ncan be transformed into\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x}{2}\n$$\n\nWriting $x$ as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x}{1}\n$$\n\nwe can multiply\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 1 2\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\dfrac x 1\n$$\n\nusing fraction multiplication, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{2}x &= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot x \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\frac{x}{1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1 \\cdot x}{2 \\cdot 1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{x}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186106960 1029 "The usual procedure also works in the reverse direction. For example, to find an equivalent expression for\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2(y+1)}5\n$$\n\nwe can separate the variable part from the rest of the fraction, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(2(y + 1))/(5) &= (2 \\cdot (y + 1))/(5 \\cdot 1) \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{5} \\cdot ((y + 1))/(1) \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{5} \\cdot (y + 1) \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{5}(y + 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186106961 1029 "Many real-world problems involve two quantities that must satisfy certain limits at the same time, like staying under a budget while mixing two items. Two-variable inequalities help model these situations.\n\nFor example, suppose Marie wants to buy some gifts for her friends. Her budget is $\\[math]90$, and she has narrowed the gifts down to two possibilities:\n\n- calendars, each costing $\\[/math]18$, and\n- battery packs, each costing $\\[math]15$.\n\nCan we form a mathematical statement describing all possible combinations of calendars and battery packs Marie can purchase?\n\nLet $c$ be the number of calendars and $b$ the number of battery packs Marie purchases. Then, the total amount (in dollars) she will pay is given by the expression\n\n$$\n18c+15b\n$$\n\nMarie's budget is $\\[/math] 90$. This means that the expression above can be no greater than $90$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n18c+15b \\leq 90\n$$\n\nWe can simplify this inequality by dividing both sides by $3$. This gives\n\n$$\n6c+5b \\leq 30\n$$"] [17592186106962 1029 "A graph is often the best way to represent the solution to a system of two-variable linear inequalities. We'll illustrate with an example.\n\nSuppose Mr. Cook is preparing cakes for a bake sale. He has a total budget of $\\[math]120$ for all the ingredients. Each chocolate cake costs $\\[/math]12$ to make, and each sponge cake costs $\\[math]6$. Let's formulate this situation as a two-variable inequality, and plot its solution on a graph.\n\nLet $x$ be the number of chocolate cakes he makes and let $y$ be the number of sponge cakes. Since he has a total budget of $\\[/math]120$, we have the following inequality:\n\n$$\n12x + 6y \\leq 120\n$$\n\nBy dividing both sides of this inequality by $6$, we get\n\n$$\n2x+y \\leq 20\n$$\n\nTo plot this inequality, we will first convert the inequality into slope-intercept form. This gives\n\n$$\ny \\le - 2x + 20\n$$\n\nLet's now draw the region.\n\n- Since the inequality involves a 'less than or equal to' sign, the line $y = -2x + 20$ is part of the solution. Therefore, we draw a solid line.\n- Then, we must decide whether to shade above or below the line. The inequality $y \\leq -2x + 20$ refers to the $y$-values that are *less than* or equal to those on the line $y = -2x +20$. So, we shade *below* the line.\n- Lastly, because $x$ and $y$ cannot be negative (we cannot bake a negative number of cakes), we limit our shading to the first quadrant.\n\nTherefore, we get the following graph:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1320/Source/Images/6b0dda286dd6e6a3511ae6a4ca17b209.png)"] [17592186106963 1029 "When modeling situations in context, the solution (i.e., the shaded part of the graph) is called the **feasible region.** Any point inside the feasible region is a potential solution to our problem.\n\nTo understand this, let's go back to the feasible region for Mr. Cook's bake sale.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1320/Source/Images/bd36f901ea40d94561120923ded604a9.png)\n\n- If Mr. Cook wants to make $5$ chocolate and $5$ sponge cakes, then our solution tells us that this is a possibility since the point $(5,5)$ lies in the feasible region:![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1320/Source/Images/c72c11a969d69dd6d4cbf29e36654148.png)\n- However, if Mr. Cook wants to make $10$ chocolate and $5$ sponge cakes, our solution tells us that this is *not* a possibility since the point $(10,5)$ does *not* lie in the feasible region:![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1320/Source/Images/1d25ac6166db3d1d268c9e62b48ae8f5.png)"] [17592186106964 1029 "The **power of product rule** states that if we want to raise the product $xy$ to some power $n$, we must raise each factor to the power $n$ and then multiply:\n\n$$\n(xy)^n=x^n y^n\n$$\n\nFor example,\n\n$$\n(xy)^4=x^4y^4\n$$\n\nTo see why the power of product rule is true, let's take the above example and work it out again the long way, changing the order of factors in the multiplication. We get the same result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(xy)^{4} &=  \\\\\n(xy)(xy)(xy)(xy) &=  \\\\\n(x \\cdot x \\cdot x \\cdot x)(y \\cdot y \\cdot y \\cdot y) &=  \\\\\n&= x^{4}y^{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe power of product rule is just a shortcut for the process above."] [17592186106965 1029 "Suppose we want to calculate $\\lim_\\limits{x \\to \\infty} x^2$ and $\\lim_\\limits{x \\to -\\infty} x^2$. To do this, we can use the graph of $y=x^2$, which takes the familiar shape of a parabola.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1263/Source/Images/bb0b4dd9154debe346e257fc87059532.png)\n\nOn the right side of the graph, we see that $y$ increases without bound as $x \\to \\infty$. On the left side of the graph, we have a similar situation: $y$ again increases without bound as $x \\to -\\infty$. Consequently, we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\to \\infty} x^2=\\infty\\qquad \\textrm{and}\\qquad \\lim_\\limits{x \\to -\\infty} x^2=\\infty\n$$\n\nNotice that the functions $y=x^4$ and $y=x^6$ have a similar shape, and as such their limiting behavior is the same.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1263/Source/Images/afc9e9d1d4cf97b908b4b97887c1fad8.png)\n\nIn general, whenever $n$ is an *even natural number*, we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\to \\infty} x^n=\\infty\\qquad \\textrm{and}\\qquad \\lim_\\limits{x \\to -\\infty} x^n=\\infty, \\qquad \\textrm{for}\\qquad n=2,4,6,\\ldots\n$$"] [17592186106966 1029 "Now let's look at the graph of $y=x^3$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1263/Source/Images/0f414490f46b606844c43d9005b8e909.png)\n\nWe notice that the situation here is different:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x \\to \\infty} x^3=\\infty \\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad \\lim_{x \\to -\\infty} x^3=-\\infty\n$$\n\nThese limits are valid for any power function of the form $x^n$, where $n$ is an *odd natural number*.\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\to \\infty} x^n=\\infty\\qquad \\textrm{and}\\qquad \\lim_\\limits{x \\to -\\infty} x^n=-\\infty, \\qquad n=1,3,5,\\ldots\n$$\n\nAgain, multiplication of $x^n$ by a positive constant does not affect the limit at infinity. However, multiplication by a negative constant changes the sign of the limit."] [17592186106967 1029 "To compute the limit at infinity of a general polynomial, we *only* need to consider the term with the largest exponent, called the **leading term**. All of the other terms can be disregarded!\n\nFor example, to compute\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\to \\infty} \\left(6x + 2x^3-7x^2+12\\right) \\,\n$$\n\nwe identify the leading term and compute its limit. Here, the leading term is ${\\color{blue}{2x^3}}$, so we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → ∞)(6x + 2x^{3} - 7x^{2} + 12) &= lim_(x → ∞)(2x^{3}) \\\\\n&= lim_(x → ∞)(x^{3}) \\\\\n&= ∞\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reason this works is that when $x$ becomes very large, the behavior of the polynomial is dominated by the term with the largest exponent.\n\n**Watch out!** The leading term is *not* always the first term that appears in the expression. Rather, it is the term *with the largest exponent*. This term may appear anywhere in the expression.\n\nThe reason why we call the term with the largest exponent the \"leading\" term is that when the polynomial is arranged in standard form (with exponents ordered from greatest to least), the term with the largest exponent appears first:\n$6x+ {\\color{blue}{2x^3}}-7x^2+12 \\quad \\to \\quad {\\color{blue}{2x^3}}-7x^2+6x+12$"] [17592186106968 1029 "In this lesson, we'll discuss some fundamental objects in geometry. The objects we'll discuss are some of the building blocks we use to describe all geometrical objects.\n\nA **point** is an exact location in space. It has no size (i.e., no length, width, or depth), only position. A point is indicated with a dot usually labeled with a capital letter (e.g., $P$, $Q$, $S$,...), as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1378/Source/Images/394131851a697a92fe0820c40e84d994.png)\n\nA **line** is a straight object that is infinitely long and has no width. We can think of it as an infinite collection of points, extending infinitely in both directions. A line has no endpoints!\n\nLet's take a look at the following line.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1378/Source/Images/cefde0211d0bfe21ca3234a1a5ec81d0.png)\n\nA line that passes through the points $P$ and $Q$ is expressed as\n$\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{PQ}$.\n\nWe use the \"$\\longleftrightarrow$\" symbol to denote that the line extends forever in **both** directions.\n\nLines can also be indicated with lowercase letters. For example:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1378/Source/Images/ea3401bcfecd7809c0a16876239a27f9.png)"] [17592186106969 1029 "A **line segment** is part of a line that's bounded by two points, called the **endpoints** of the segment. A line segment with endpoints $A$ and $B$, like the one shown below, is denoted by $\\overline{AB}$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1379/Source/Images/f9b70e5dc420cc9bf5ecc0d6601cae4a.png)\n\nThe **measure** of a line segment is its length. For a line segment $\\overline{AB}$, the measure is denoted by $AB$ (with no line on top) to distinguish it from the line segment itself.\n\n**Watch out!** The line segment $\\overline{AB}$ and its measure $AB$ are *not* the same thing. The line segment $\\overline{AB}$ is a geometric object, while its measure $AB$ (with no line on top) is a number. It's similar to how a person is a physical entity, while their height is a number.\n\n**Note:** We could also express the segment as $\\overline{BA}$, switching the order of the endpoints. The notations $\\overline{BA}$ and $\\overline{AB}$ represent the same exact segment. We could also write the length of the segment as $BA$ or $AB$."] [17592186106970 1029 "Remember that longer sides in a triangle are opposite to larger angles, and vice versa. But what if two (or more) sides have the same length?\n\nAs you might have guessed, if two sides have the same length, then the opposite angles have the same measure. And vice-versa: if two angles have the same measure, then the opposite sides have the same length.\n\nSo, we obtain the following important properties of isosceles and equilateral triangles:\n\n- A triangle is **equilateral** if and only if all of its angles are of $60^\\circ$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1403/Source/Images/3ff8fa10e5b43b23287dfb0fba9ac84d.png)\n\n- A triangle is **isosceles** if and only if it has two congruent angles.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1403/Source/Images/80c70e314a4259ba9e4e4a1ab0d997d5.png)\n\n**Note:** The statement above encompasses two theorems:\n\n- The **isosceles triangle theorem**:*If a triangle is isosceles, then the base angles are congruent.*\n- The **converse isosceles triangle theorem**: *If two angles in a triangle have the same measure, then the triangle must be isosceles.*"] [17592186106971 1029 "Consider the triangle $\\triangle ABC$ shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1386/Source/Images/63b1af2c2fb0f5e13c23c2f223a8475d.png)\n\nThe sum of the length of its sides is called the **perimeter**. Here, the perimeter of our triangle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\np &= AB + BC + AC \\\\\n&= 2 + 4.2 + 3.8 \\\\\n&= 10cm\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186106972 1029 "Suppose we have a quadrilateral $ABCD$ as shown below and it is known that\n\n$$\n{AD} = 6\\,\\mathrm{m}\n$$\n\n$$\n{AB} = 2\\,\\mathrm{m}\n$$\n\n$$\n{BC} = 4\\,\\mathrm{m}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nCD=5\\,\\mathrm{m}\n$$\n\nWhat is the perimeter of the quadrilateral?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1386/Source/Images/2b2b349522ae34309a465eab99d2f36f.png)\n\nRemember that the **perimeter** $p$ of a polygon is the sum of the measures of all its sides. So, we can simply calculate\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\np &= AD + AB + BC + CD \\\\\n&= 6 + 2 + 4 + 5 \\\\\n&= 17m\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186106973 1029 "Let's take a look at the polygons depicted below and find their perimeters.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1386/Source/Images/8407f61d141b39b655c326978cddc7b2.png)\n\nFrom the picture, we conclude that polygons I, II and III all have a perimeter of $10$ while polygon IV has a perimeter of $8$.\n\nPolygons that have the same perimeter are called **isoperimetric**. So I, II and III are isoperimetric polygons."] [17592186106974 1029 "We'll explore some properties of parallelograms in this lesson. Let's begin by stating the definition of a parallelogram.\n\n> *A parallelogram is a quadrilateral where the opposite sides are parallel.*\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1354/Source/Images/ac3c2d89635dbc0d71c0bf368986bbd6.png)\n\nIt's possible to prove the following property.\n\n> *In a parallelogram, the opposite sides are congruent.*\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1354/Source/Images/a12cf95048a7f12ad650f9b2bf7bb8fb.png)\n\nThe fact that the opposite sides are congruent is a *consequence* of the definition of a parallelogram, not part of the definition itself. We'll learn how to prove this in future lessons."] [17592186106975 1029 "All parallelograms have the following property.\n\n> *The diagonals of a parallelogram bisect each other.*\n\nIn other words, the first diagonal of a parallelogram bisects the second, and the second diagonal bisects the first, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1354/Source/Images/5f9f0b2dac02f10fedb154a4377511fe.png)\n\nWe'll prove this property in a future lesson."] [17592186106976 1029 "The **area** of a shape is a measure of how much space is enclosed by a shape on a plane. Let's consider a square with sides of length of $1\\,\\textrm{in}$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1352/Source/Images/c1f5c41cde345efbe34e0e3e489bec4f.png)\n\nThe area of this square is equal to ${\\color{red}{1\\,\\textrm{in}^2}}$, which we say in words as \"one square inch.\"\n\nSimilarly, if we have three squares with sides of length $1\\,\\textrm{cm}$, $1\\,\\textrm{m}$, and $1\\,\\textrm{ft}$ respectively, then their respective areas will be\n$1\\,\\textrm{cm}^2, \\qquad 1\\,\\textrm{m}^2, \\qquad 1\\,\\textrm{ft}^2$."] [17592186106977 1029 "Let's now suppose that we would like to compute the area of the square $ABCD$, shown below. Notice that, this time, the sides of the square span multiple unit lengths.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1352/Source/Images/14a3476d8210a24903e91d3c4a4e5588.png)\n\nThe length of one side of a grid square is equal to $1\\,\\textrm{cm}$. So, the area of one grid square is $1\\,\\textrm{cm}^2$.\n\nNow, we need to find how many grid squares are contained inside $ABCD$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1352/Source/Images/62cf21402f7c1c077ee21f03cbdb07b5.png)\n\nInside the large blue square, there are $3$ rows containing $3$ grid squares each, so we can fit exactly\n\n$$\n3 \\times 3 = 3^2 = 9\n$$\n\ngrid squares inside $ABCD$.\n\nEach grid square has area $1\\,\\textrm{cm}^2$, so the area $\\mathcal A$ of $ABCD$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A} = (3\\,\\textrm{cm})^2 = 9\\,\\textrm{cm}^2\n$$\n\nIn general, if we have a square with a side of length $a$, then the area of the square is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A} = a^2\n$$"] [17592186106978 1029 "The **area of a parallelogram** is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A} = b \\cdot h\n$$\n\nwhere $b$ is the **base** and $h$ is the **height** of the parallelogram. The height of a parallelogram is the length of a perpendicular segment drawn from the base to the opposite side.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's use the above formula to compute the area of the parallelogram $ABCD$ shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1399/Source/Images/ac2831ef2590492f083c173c5556a742.png)\n\nFrom the picture above, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= b \\cdot h \\\\\n&= 7 \\cdot 5 \\\\\n&= 35ft^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186106979 1029 "In general, for a rectangle with sides of length $a$ and $b$, the area of the rectangle is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A} = a \\cdot b\n$$\n\nLet's demonstrate by finding the area of the rectangle $PQRS$, shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1352/Source/Images/81384f1259fb79152447a7be5c30b3b5.png)\n\nFrom the figure, we see that the rectangle has the following side lengths:\n\n$$\na = {PQ} = 10\\,\\mathrm{cm}, \\qquad b = {PS} = 6\\,\\mathrm{cm}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= a \\cdot b \\\\\n&= 10cm \\cdot 6cm \\\\\n&= 60cm^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIt does not matter which side we call $a$ and which side we call $b$. In the above example, if we set\n\n$$\na = {QR} = 6\\,\\mathrm{cm}, \\qquad b = {RS} = 10\\,\\mathrm{cm}\n$$\n\nthen we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= a \\cdot b \\\\\n&= 10cm \\cdot 6cm \\\\\n&= 60cm^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas before."] [17592186106980 1029 "The **area of a triangle** can be computed using the formula\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A} = \\dfrac{b \\cdot h}{2}\n$$\n\nwhere $b$ is a *base* of the triangle and $h$ is the corresponding *height*.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's compute the area of the triangle with base\n$b = 4$\nand the corresponding height is\n$h = 8$,\nas shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1397/Source/Images/fc113e09eff8eb7443efb28ada750225.png)\n\nThe area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= \\frac{b \\cdot h}{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{4 \\cdot 8}{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{32}{2} \\\\\n&= 16\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** At the end of the lesson, we'll demonstrate where the formula comes from. But first, let's get some practice using it."] [17592186106981 1029 "The **area of a trapezoid** is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A} = \\dfrac{(a+b)\\cdot h}{2}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $a$ and $b$ are lengths of the **bases,** and\n- $h$ is the **height** of the trapezoid (the length of a perpendicular segment drawn between the bases).\n\nWe'll derive this result at the end of the lesson.\n\nTo demonstrate how to apply this formula, let's use the above formula to compute the area of the trapezoid $ABCD$ shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1353/Source/Images/2d351d83ef2d6c3020212b693227841c.png)\n\nFrom the picture above, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= ((a + b) \\cdot h)/(2) \\\\\n&= ((AB + CD) \\cdot CH)/(2) \\\\\n&= ((6 + 4) \\cdot 3.5)/(2) \\\\\n&= \\frac{10 \\cdot 3.5}{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{35}{2} \\\\\n&= 17.5cm^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186106982 1029 "We know that the area formula for a triangle is\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A} = \\dfrac{b \\cdot h}{2}\n$$\n\nwhere $b$ is a base and $h$ is the corresponding height.\n\nBut where does this formula come from?\n\nTo understand where the formula comes from, consider $\\triangle{ABC}$ below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1397/Source/Images/ca6f0f235d553fa3278d1726334102fc.png)\n\nLet's draw a rectangle around $\\triangle{ABC}$, as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1397/Source/Images/2ccb505e1fe94085d7ed416d4fcfedd6.png)\n\nWe have two pairs of right triangles with equal areas (each pair is indicated with arrows).\n\nSo, the area of $\\triangle{ABC}$ is exactly half of the area of the rectangle. This gives\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A}_{ABC} = \\dfrac{AB \\cdot CH}{2}\n$$"] [17592186106983 1029 "Suppose we know that the triangles $ABC$ and $NLM$ shown below are similar such that $\\triangle ABC\\sim\\triangle NLM$. How do we find the length of $\\overline{NL}$?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1367/Source/Images/64cc605ac09916a50ca8987ce707bbe6.png)\n\nAccording to the similarity statement, $\\triangle ABC\\sim \\triangle NLM$. Therefore,\n\n- $\\overline{AB}$ corresponds to $\\overline{NL}$,\n- $\\overline{BC}$ corresponds to $\\overline{LM}$,\n- $\\overline{AC}$ corresponds to $\\overline{NM}$.\n\nIn similar polygons, the ratio of any two corresponding sides are in proportion. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{AC}{NM} &= \\frac{AB}{NL} \\\\\n\\frac{12}{9} &= \\frac{20}{NL} \\\\\n\\frac{9}{12} &= \\frac{NL}{20} \\\\\nNL &= \\frac{20 \\cdot 9}{12} \\\\\nNL &= 15\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"]}
